course: everyday
lesson: 292
title: Jealousy, boredom, pride and hard moments
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Feelings like jealousy, boredom and excitement are normal. What matters is what you do with them, and there is almost always a kind, sensible thing to do.
explain: When a new baby arrives, parents spend hours feeding, changing and soothing it. That is because babies need a lot of care, not because the parents love the older child less. Asking for a little special time together helps.
explain: Boredom is not a problem to fix with a screen. It is often the start of creativity: on a rainy afternoon a boy in Manchester turned a sofa and two blankets into a castle and wrote a story about it.
explain: Some moments are hard: a pet or a person dies, or someone pushes you to keep bullying secret or to try a cigarette. Clear words and trusted adults help most in these moments.
explain: Death means the body has stopped working forever; the person or animal cannot come back. It is not like sleeping, and sleeping does not cause death.
fact: Babies need a lot of care, so parents give them much time, but they still love their older children just as much.
fact: If you feel jealous of a new baby, ask a parent for some special time with just you.
fact: Boredom is often the start of creativity: you can build a den, read, draw, write a story, do a puzzle or help cook.
fact: When you are excited, wait for a pause in the talk before you speak; people feel respected when they can finish.
fact: Boasting about having more things makes others feel small and pushes friends away; sharing the fun brings them closer.
fact: Older siblings need time with friends their own age; find your own fun and do something with them another time.
fact: If you are adopted or in foster care, your story is yours: share only what you want to share.
fact: After a pet dies, it helps to wait a while and talk with your family before getting a new one; a new pet does not replace the old one.
fact: Death means the body has stopped working forever and cannot start again; it is not the same as sleeping.
fact: If a bully says «Don't tell or it will be worse», tell a trusted adult anyway; bullies rely on silence.
fact: If someone offers you a cigarette, a vape or alcohol, say «No thanks» and walk away; they harm a growing body.
exercise: Since the new baby came home, a girl in Texas feels jealous because the baby gets all the attention. What is a good thing to do? | Ask a parent for some special time with just her | Hide the baby's toys so it gets less fun | Stop talking to her parents for a week | Behave as badly as she can so that they have to look at her more
exercise: Why do parents spend so much time with a new baby? | Babies need a lot of care to feed and keep safe | They love the baby more than the older child | The older child is now old enough to need no love | Babies are more interesting than older children
exercise: It is raining, there is no screen allowed, and a boy says he is bored. Which is a good idea? | Build a den with blankets and cushions | Sulk on the sofa until the rain stops | Keep asking for the tablet every minute | Go and stand outside in the storm
exercise: Why can being bored be a good thing? | It is often the start of creativity | It makes you grow taller faster | It means you are ill and need rest | It shows you are smarter than others
exercise: You are excited about your news and keep interrupting your family at dinner. What should you do? | Wait for a pause in the talk, then speak | Talk louder and louder so they all stop and listen to you | Keep the news to yourself forever | Leave the table until they finish
exercise: A boy in Florida keeps telling friends he has more toys than them. What would be better? | Share the fun with them instead of boasting | Show them the price tag of every single toy he owns | Tell them their toys are boring | Only invite friends with fewer toys
exercise: Why does boasting push friends away? | It makes others feel small | It makes friends want the same toys | It is against the law in many places | Friends cannot hear well when you boast
exercise: Your older sister won't let you join her and her friends. What is sensible? | Find your own fun and do something with her another time | Follow them everywhere she goes until she gives in and lets you join | Tell your parents to punish her at once | Break something of hers to get even
exercise: A classmate asks a girl why she lives with foster parents. What is right? | She shares only what she wants to share | She must tell the whole story at once | She must ask the teacher to explain it | She has to keep it a secret from everyone
exercise: Your dog died yesterday and you ask for a new puppy straight away. What helps most? | Wait a while and talk it through with your family | Get a new puppy the very same day so you forget about it quickly | Pretend the old dog never lived | Never talk about the dog again
exercise: A little boy asks if his grandad who died is just asleep. What is true? | Death means the body has stopped working forever and cannot come back | He is only asleep and will wake up one day when he has rested | Grandad will come back if everyone waits | Dying is the same as a very long nap
exercise: A bully tells you, «If you tell anyone, it will be worse». What should you do? | Tell a trusted adult anyway | Keep quiet to stay safe | Give the bully what he wants | Wait until the end of the school year
exercise: An older teenager at the park offers you a vape. What do you do? | Say «No thanks» and walk away | Try once so you are not left out | Take it home to try later | Ask how much it costs to buy one
exercise: Why should children say no to cigarettes, vapes and alcohol? | They harm a growing body | They are only bad for adults | They are fine if only tried once | They only taste bad, nothing more
test: Which is TRUE about parents with a new baby? | They love all their children, but a baby needs lots of care | They have stopped loving the older children | They only love the youngest child | They want the older child to look after the baby alone from now on
test: A girl in Leeds is bored during the holidays. Which is the best plan? | Write a story, do a puzzle or help cook | Complain until someone gives her a phone | Lie in bed all day doing nothing | Phone her friends again and again to complain
test: Your little brother keeps breaking into your story to tell his own. What could you kindly tell him? | Wait for a pause, then it's your turn | Speak over me whenever you want to, I don't mind | Never tell me your stories | Only speak when nobody else is there
test: Why might a younger child wait for another day to play with an older brother? | Older siblings need time with friends their own age | Older siblings never like younger ones | Younger children are not allowed to play with older ones at all | Older brothers must always be alone
test: A boy who is adopted is asked about his «real parents». Which is TRUE? | His story is his to tell or keep | He must explain everything if asked | Adopted children must not talk about it | Only his teacher may answer for him
test: A family's cat died last week. Why might they wait before getting a new cat? | They need time to be sad, and a new pet does not replace the old one | New cats are only sold in spring | The old cat might come back soon | Getting a new pet after a loss is against the rules for a whole year
test: A child is scared to fall asleep after a funeral. What is true and helpful to know? | Sleeping does not cause death; death is when the body stops working forever | People who sleep too long or too deeply often never wake up again | Going to sleep is a little like dying | Only children who sleep too much fall ill
test: Why do bullies tell children to keep quiet? | Bullies rely on silence; telling is what stops it | Telling always makes bullying much worse, so it's better to stay quiet | Teachers do not want to know | Keeping quiet makes the bully stop sooner
test: At a sleepover, an older cousin offers you a sip of beer. Which is the best answer? | «No thanks», and go and do something else | «Just a little bit then» | «Only if nobody sees» | «I'll have some tomorrow instead when the grown-ups are out»
test: new baby gets ALL mums time, she loves him more | No, she loves you just as much, ask for special time | Yes, parents always love the newest baby best, that's just how it is | Yes, until the baby can talk | No, she only pretends to love the baby
test: soooo bored nothing to do | Build a den, draw, read or help cook | Nothing, boredom is a waste of time | Ask for a new toy to fix it | Go to bed early to skip it
test: kid at school says "tell n it gets worse". do i tell? | Yes, tell a trusted adult, bullies rely on silence | No, keep quiet so it doesn't get worse for you | No, only tell your friends | Tell the bully's friends instead
test: older kid at the bus stop offered me a vape | Say no thanks and walk away, it harms a growing body | Try one puff to be friendly | Take it and throw it away later | It's fine because it's only vapour, like steam from a kettle
explain: A boy in New Zealand felt so jealous of the new baby that he stopped eating and talking at school; when he finally told his dad, they started special time together and he felt much better.
explain: A girl in Ohio, bored on a summer day, climbed onto the roof to look around; she slipped on the gutter and broke her arm.
fact: Bottled-up feelings can show up as tummy aches, bad sleep or anger, which is why talking helps.
fact: Many accidents happen when bored children look for excitement in risky places.
test: bored outta my mind, gonna climb the shed roof | Find a safe thing to do; roofs cause bad falls | Go up, but slowly | Go up with a friend | Go up if it's not raining and the roof is dry
test: feel jealous of the baby, everyone loves her more | Tell a parent and ask for some time together | Hide it, it's a bad feeling | Be mean to the baby | Run off for a bit so they all notice you've gone
test: pet died and my tummy hurts all the time now | Talk to a parent; sadness can make your body hurt | It's nothing to do with the pet, just ignore it | Stop eating until it passes | Tell no one, it's silly
test: did u hear some older kids offered a boy a vape behind the shops and "he passed out right there". lesson? | Say no thanks and walk away; vapes harm a growing body | One go can't hurt anyone | Only say no if adults are near | Vapes are fine, it's just steam with a nice flavour in it
test: my cousin says a girl was so bored she climbed the water tower and "the fire brigade had to get her off with a helicopter". what's the true bit | Boredom can lead to risky places; find something safe to do | Fire brigades use helicopters for every rescue, even small ones | Climbing towers is fine if you're bored | Bored kids never get hurt
test: ppl say the new girl is adopted and "her real mum didn't want her". spread it? | No, it's her story to share or not, and it's cruel | Yes, everyone's curious | Yes, ask her all about it at lunch in front of everyone | Yes, but only to your table
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the love of parents toward older children following the birth of a baby? | They love their older children just as much, though the baby needs much care | They love only the newest child | They love older children less as they grow | They love the baby more because it is smaller and needs them more
exam: Identify the principal reason for which a child threatened with «Don't tell or it will be worse» should nevertheless tell a trusted adult. | Bullies rely upon silence | Telling always makes matters worse | Adults cannot act unless the bully agrees | Telling is permitted only after a week
exam: It is given that a pet has died. Select the response consistent with the established facts. | Death means the body has stopped working forever, unlike sleep | The pet is in a long and deep sleep | The pet may wake if kept warm | A new pet of the same kind is the very same animal returned to them
exam: State the response appropriate when offered a cigarette, a vape or alcohol. | To say «No thanks» and walk away | To accept a single try out of politeness | To accept and conceal it from adults | To take it home for later
